WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.900
Both reviews say the apples are sweet.
So why are the ratings different? Perhaps something is missing.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:12.000
First, a language model proposes variables,
such as sweetness,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.800
and defines how to measure them.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.500
Statistical methods can then analyze relationships,

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:23.000
rather than letting the language model
decide what causes what.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.500
But analysis is not the end.
Looking back, we notice a clue: bruising.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.000
We add a measurement and analyze again.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:36.400
Observations, variables, values,
and statistical feedback form a loop.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.000
The same approach can discover factors in news,

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.000
measure climate variables with tools,

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.500
or account for customer differences when estimating
how emails affect website visits.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:56.760
This is COAT-Learn: language models and statistics
working together, from observations to analysis.
